/-!
# Systems of parameters of a local ring

Let `(R, m)` be a Noetherian local ring of dimension `d`. An *ideal of definition* of `R` is an
ideal `I` with `√I = m` [StackProject 07DU]. A *system of parameters* of `R` is a sequence of `d`
elements of `m` which generates an ideal of definition of `R`. By the dimension theorem
[StackProject 00KQ] no ideal of definition of `R` can be generated by fewer than `d` elements, and
some is generated by `d` of them, so systems of parameters exist and are exactly the shortest
generating sequences of ideals of definition. If the maximal ideal `m` is itself generated by `d`
elements then `R` is called a *regular local ring* and such a sequence a *regular system of
parameters*.

## Main definitions

* `IsLocalRing.IsSystemOfParameters`: a list `rs` of elements of a local ring `R` is a system of
  parameters of `R` when the ideal it generates has radical `m` and it has `dim R` entries.

## Implementation notes

The source's definition has two items, one predicate on the ring and two notions of a *sequence*.
The predicate of item (2), "`R` is a regular local ring", is Mathlib's `IsRegularLocalRing`, which
already carries `@[stacks 00KU]` and is not restated here: it is spelled
`(maximalIdeal R).spanFinrank = ringKrullDim R`, i.e. `m` is generated by `dim R` elements, which
is the source's condition read through the minimal number of generators. The remaining notion of
that item, a *regular* system of parameters, is the conjunction
`Ideal.ofList rs = maximalIdeal R ∧ ringKrullDim R = rs.length`, whose first conjunct already
implies the radical condition below; it is a composite with no call site of its own yet, so no
second name is introduced for it.

A sequence is a `List R`, the form in which Mathlib states the conditions a system of parameters is
asked to satisfy downstream — `RingTheory.Sequence.IsWeaklyRegular` and
`RingTheory.Sequence.IsRegular`, and
`IsLocalRing.isRegular_iff_isWeaklyRegular_of_subset_maximalIdeal` — so that the two notions
compose without a reindexing; and `Ideal.ofList`, Mathlib's ideal generated by a list, is the ideal
the source writes `(x_1, …, x_d)`. Repetitions are allowed by the spelling, as they are by the
source's, and `rs.length` is the source's `d`. The source's requirement `x_i ∈ m` is not a conjunct
because it is implied:
`Ideal.ofList rs ≤ (Ideal.ofList rs).radical = m`. Being an ideal of definition is written
`I.radical = maximalIdeal R`, as in [StackProject 00K8] and [StackProject 00KQ], Tag 07DU having no
definition of its own here. Noetherianness of `R` is not assumed, the definition needing only `m`
and `dim R`; it is over a Noetherian local ring that it carries the content the source ascribes to
it, since there `dim R < ∞` and a system of parameters exists.

Faithfulness to the source, point by point. (1) No hypothesis is idle: `IsLocalRing R` provides the
`m` that both conjuncts speak of, and the two conjuncts are the source's two demands on
`x_1, …, x_d` — that they generate an ideal of definition and that there are `dim R` of them.
(2) The scalars and base are the source's: `Ideal.ofList rs` is the ideal of `R` generated by the
entries of `rs`, its radical is taken in `R`, and `ringKrullDim R` is the dimension of `R` itself —
no quotient, completion or localization intervenes. (3) The source names no canonical map here;
what it pins and what the definition pins is the *number* of elements, `dim R` exactly, rather than
finitely many or at least `dim R`. (4) The instantiation is not degenerate: over `R = k[[t]]`, of
dimension `1`, the sequence `[t]` is a system of parameters and so is `[t ^ 2]`, which generates an
ideal of definition other than `m`; a field admits `[]`; and `[t, 0]` over `k[[t]]` is not one,
having two entries.

## References

* [StackProject 00KU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00KU)
